---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="Your Guidebook | Queerness Experiences" description={"Your queer Tulum guidebook."} noindex={true}>

<div class="gb-wrap">
  <div class="gb-inner">

    <header class="gb-cover">
      <div class="gb-cover-eye">Queerness Experiences Presents</div>
      <h1 class="gb-cover-title">Tulum</h1>
      <div class="gb-cover-rule"></div>
      <p class="gb-cover-sub">A local's favorites, curated for queer &amp; trans travelers</p>
      <div class="gb-cover-tags">
        Coffee · Cenotes · Beach Clubs · Nightlife<br>
        Wellness · Hidden Gems · Safety Tips · Community Spaces
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="gb-sec">
      <h2 class="gb-h1">Welcome to Tulum.</h2>
      <div class="gb-meta">By Santi Solís · @queerness_experiences_tulum</div>
      <p class="gb-italic">Born in Mexico. Raised in the U.S. UCLA wildlife biologist. Came home to Mexico, built a life in Tulum.</p>
      <p class="gb-body">This is a list of my favorites, as a queer local. Every recommendation in these pages is somewhere I have personally been, eaten at, danced in, or trusted with my money and my body.</p>
      <p class="gb-body">If anything is out of date, or you need a rec that is not here, reach out. I am always happy to help.</p>

      <div class="gb-callout">
        <div class="gb-callout-k">Want to experience Tulum with a local queer guide?</div>
        <div class="gb-callout-s">Secret cenotes, food tours, jungle hikes, and more — small group tours led by locals who know every corner of this town.</div>
        <a href="/tours" class="gb-callout-u">queernessexperiences.com</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="gb-sec">
      <div class="gb-band">Money &amp; Getting Around</div>

      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">ATMs, Don't Get Dinged</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Santander recommended, avoid BBVA and tourist strip ATMs</div>
        <p class="gb-body">Almost every ATM in Tulum will try to charge you a 5 to 15 percent currency conversion fee. You can say no. When the machine asks if you want to accept the conversion, decline. My go to is Santander, reliable, usually no extra fees.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Currency Exchange</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Bring your passport, Citibanamex recommended</div>
        <p class="gb-body">Exchanging USD cash? Bring your passport, most exchange offices require it by law. My favorite is Citibanamex in the city center, consistently the best rates in Tulum.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Carlos, My Trusted Driver</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">WhatsApp +52 984 213 638, tell him Queerness Experiences sent you</div>
        <p class="gb-body">Tulum does not have Uber. Taxis can be steep if you do not know what is fair. I always message Carlos first, the fairest and most consistent prices in Tulum.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Eiby Taxi App</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Download before you arrive, negotiate price before the ride</div>
        <p class="gb-body">Negotiate your price before the ride, no surprises, no haggling. The driver comes to your exact location.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Paying by Card, Always Choose Pesos</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Decline the dollar conversion every time</div>
        <p class="gb-body">When you pay by card anywhere in Tulum, the machine will often ask if you want to be charged in pesos or in your home currency, usually dollars. Always choose pesos. Your own credit card already converts the currency at its own rate, which is almost always better, and that conversion fee on the machine is an extra charge stacked on top, not a convenience.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="gb-sec">
      <div class="gb-band">Coffee</div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Story Horse Cafe</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Local favorite, specialty Mexican coffee, Tulum Centro</div>
        <p class="gb-body">One of the best coffee shops in Tulum, full stop. Single origin beans from small producers across Mexico, every cup is a little geography lesson. They also host a run club Saturday mornings. Find them on Avenida Kukulcan in Tulum Centro.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Vaiven</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">La Veleta</div>
        <p class="gb-body">A solid coffee spot in La Veleta, good for when you are already on that side of town.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="gb-sec">
      <div class="gb-band">Where to Eat</div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Negro Huitlacoche</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Vegan, vegetarian, GF options, jam nights Mondays</div>
        <p class="gb-body">My favorite restaurant in Tulum. Rooted in Mexican tradition with a creative twist. Order the corn. Monday nights are a local institution, live jam sessions with the best musicians in town.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Mestixa Tulum</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Local obsession, book ahead</div>
        <p class="gb-body">I have friends who fly to Tulum specifically to eat here. The baos are extraordinary. The whole menu is thoughtful, beautiful, and worth every peso.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Veleta Market</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Multi vendor market, events and parties, opened 2024</div>
        <p class="gb-body">Part food hall, part community hangout, part party venue. Do not leave without trying the pasta from Ciao Bella inside. Follow their Instagram for events.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Burrito Amor</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Vegan and vegetarian options, around for over 10 years</div>
        <p class="gb-body">A Tulum fixture for more than a decade, which says everything in a town where most restaurants do not last two years. Good burritos, vegan and vegetarian options, a reliable go to.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Ruda Smash Burgers</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Opens 6pm, beyond burger and cauliflower options</div>
        <p class="gb-body">Opens at 6pm and immediately becomes the best decision of your day. Crispy edges, perfect smash. Get there before the line.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Otti</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Cacao bar, vegan friendly</div>
        <p class="gb-body">The cacao here will change your relationship with chocolate. My order is the immune boost with peanut butter and almond milk, served hot. Do not sleep on the Almondo Frappe.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="gb-sec">
      <div class="gb-band">Bars &amp; Nightlife</div>
      <p class="gb-note">The best parties in Tulum are restaurants that transform into dance floors after hours. Follow local Instagram accounts, not travel blogs.</p>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">La Guarida</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Vegan, vegetarian, GF, live music most nights, classes and events</div>
        <p class="gb-body">This is my bar. Part community center, part restaurant, part live music venue, all heart. Live bands, painting classes, theater, music workshops. If you only go to one bar in Tulum, make it this one.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">La Pizzine</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Two locations, La Veleta and the center, follow IG for party dates</div>
        <p class="gb-body">Both locations throw regular parties. Check their Instagram for the next date.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Veleta Market, After Hours</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Events vary, follow on IG</div>
        <p class="gb-body">Same spot as the food hall above, but after dark it becomes a proper party.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Mera Room</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Electronic, every two weeks or so</div>
        <p class="gb-body">For the electronic music heads. Deeper, moodier, more underground than most Tulum parties.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Palma Central, Tuesday Salsa Night</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Tuesdays, 50 peso cover includes one drink, all levels welcome</div>
        <p class="gb-body">Classes start early, dancing goes late. The crowd can hit 500 plus on a good night. One of the most joyful, unpretentious nights out in Tulum.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Negro Huitlacoche, Monday Jam Night</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Mondays, free</div>
        <p class="gb-body">The best local musicians show up Monday nights for an open jam session. Beautiful, spontaneous, free.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Sariela Camargo, Live Music</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Guitar and singing, @sariela_camargo</div>
        <p class="gb-body">Sariela is one of the most soulful live musicians I know in Tulum, guitar and voice both. If you catch her playing somewhere, stop and listen. Follow her to know where she is performing next.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Parties, Who to Follow</h3>
        <p class="gb-body">Follow Karla Mercedes to know where the parties are. She is one of the best DJs in Tulum, gets booked constantly, and plays a lot at Asian Bodega among other spots around town. She is queer and awesome. Her feed is basically a live party calendar. Find her at @karlamercedes.music</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="gb-sec">
      <div class="gb-band">Wellness &amp; Community</div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Om Collective</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Donation based, yoga and sound healing</div>
        <p class="gb-body">Yoga for everyone means exactly that. Donation based classes, sound healing, and more, no wellness industry price gouging. A real community space.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Holistika</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Yoga, meditation, sound healing, restaurant and hotel</div>
        <p class="gb-body">A full wellness center with yoga, meditation, sound healing, a restaurant, and a hotel. Genuinely good.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Pausa y Danza</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Meal prep, private chef, cooking classes, monthly community dinners</div>
        <p class="gb-body">I have been using their meal prep service for over five years. The care in every dish is palpable. They also host no phone community dinners once a month, one of the most nourishing experiences in town.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="gb-sec">
      <div class="gb-band">Things I Love to Do</div>
      <p class="gb-note">Not tourist activities. These are the people and places I go to myself, my friends, my community.</p>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Tulum Ice Club, Breathwork and Ice Bath</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Community experience, led by Chris</div>
        <p class="gb-body">Meeting Chris, the breathwork guide here, was a turning point for me. The ice bath plus breathwork combo sounds intense, because it is, but the community that shows up is warm, welcoming, and wonderful. One of the hidden gems of Tulum.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Atrevidas, Dance Classes</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Latin rhythms, reggaeton, contemporary, Monday through Thursday plus private events</div>
        <p class="gb-body">Lula runs Atrevidas, a dance school and production company. Latin rhythms, reggaeton, contemporary, plus workshops including striptease and gogo dancing, booked privately. An extremely queer friendly space. No judgment, full energy, completely liberating. Find her at @atrevidas.productora or WhatsApp +52 984 213 6715.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Cactus Ceramics, Studio Classes</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">All levels, run by Nadia</div>
        <p class="gb-body">Nadia, the owner, is one of the kindest people in Tulum. She has run ceramics classes for a local kids nonprofit for years and built a beautiful studio oasis in the process. Intimate, unhurried, genuinely special.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Tulum Surf Movement, Surf Classes</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">All levels, book ahead</div>
        <p class="gb-body">I got obsessed with learning to surf here. Great instruction, patient teachers, and the coast is perfect for beginners. Book in advance, they fill up.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Clemence Drouard, Live Drawing Sessions</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Private booking, @clemencedrouard</div>
        <p class="gb-body">Think of it like a boudoir photo shoot, but instead of a camera, Clemence draws you. You book a private session, you are the subject, and she creates the artwork live in front of you. Intimate, artistic, and unlike anything else in Tulum.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="gb-sec">
      <div class="gb-band">Cenotes</div>
      <p class="gb-note">Tulum is surrounded by cenotes, natural sinkholes filled with crystal clear freshwater. They are sacred, stunning, and very easy to visit.</p>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Cenote Escondido</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Bikeable, 150 pesos for one or 300 for both, bring cash in pesos</div>
        <p class="gb-body">My favorite for accessibility, you can bike here. Here is what Google Maps gets wrong: navigate to Cenote Cristal, pay the entrance there, then cross the highway. Escondido is right on the other side.</p>
        <div class="gb-tip">Bring cash in pesos. Most cenotes do not take cards, and the ATM near the entrance has bad rates.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Cenote Encantado</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">On the beach road, easy to combine with a beach day</div>
        <p class="gb-body">Right on the beach road, which makes it one of the easiest cenotes to add onto a beach day without going out of your way.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="gb-sec">
      <div class="gb-band">Beach Clubs</div>
      <p class="gb-note">Most of Tulum's famous beach clubs charge mandatory minimums, valet fees, and tourist prices. These do not, or at least they are honest about what you are paying for.</p>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Ikal Beach Club, The Local Favorite</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Best if you're already in the National Park, no minimum, reservation required for car or moto</div>
        <p class="gb-body">This is where Tulum locals actually go. The most reasonable prices on the coast, and the vibe is unmatched. If you are arriving by car or motorcycle, you need a reservation to enter the park, get one at ikaltulumhotel.com/access-registration before you arrive. Walking or biking in does not require a reservation.</p>
        <div class="gb-tip">Navigate to the Tulum National Park entrance on Ave Coba, not the ruins entrance. If driving or on a moto, show your reservation at the gate. Entrance is 120 pesos. If arriving with a group, have everyone except the driver say they are walking in, you pay one entrance fee and pick them up just inside.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Dos Ceibas</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">No minimum, beach beds available, queer friendly crowd, two for one drinks after 5pm</div>
        <p class="gb-body">The pizzas are delicious and land between 200 and 300 pesos. Get there early for a beach bed. Queer friendly crowd, great energy, and two for one drinks after 5pm.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Delek</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">No minimum, no parking, relaxed vibe</div>
        <p class="gb-body">Calm, quiet, fewer people. No parking, but easy on a moto or bike. Good for a chill afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gb-callout" style="text-align:left;">
        <div class="gb-callout-k" style="font-size:16px;">National Park Rules</div>
        <div class="gb-callout-s">Open 8am to 5pm. Beach only entrance is 120 pesos, the full park fee is 400 plus pesos. Walking or biking in is free. No plastic or speakers allowed, they may check your bag. Bike rental just inside the entrance is 120 pesos for the day.</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="gb-sec">
      <div class="gb-band">Giving Back</div>
      <div class="gb-entry">
        <h3 class="gb-h2">Los Amigos de la Esquina</h3>
        <div class="gb-meta">Community nonprofit, Mayan and Mexican youth programs</div>
        <p class="gb-body">A nonprofit that runs classes and workshops for local Mayan and Mexican kids. One of the most meaningful organizations in Tulum. If you have time, capacity, or resources to support them, they are worth it.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="gb-sec gb-closing">
      <div class="gb-callout">
        <div class="gb-callout-k" style="font-size:22px;">Everything in this guide, I can take you there.</div>
        <div class="gb-callout-s">Small group tours led by queer locals who know every corner of this town.<br>Secret cenotes, food tours, jungle hikes, and more.</div>
        <a href="/tours" class="gb-callout-u">Book directly at queernessexperiences.com</a>
      </div>
      <p class="gb-thanks">Thank you for supporting queer owned, locally rooted travel.<br>See you in Tulum.</p>
    </section>

  </div>
</div>

</Layout>

<style define:vars={{}}>
